
Steps:

1. Run 'StandardDataCreation.m'. This will return a file named 'StandardData.mat'. 
Description: This contains the the time-series values of the four dynamical variables Vstd, Mstd, Nstd, and Hstd, when the system is on the stable limit cycle producing
repeated action potentials. It also contains the steady-state associated values the variables Vo, Mo, No and Ho. These will be used to track if the system 
post=perturbation returned to the limit cycle or collapse into the steady state. Moreover, if it returns to the limit cycle, we can find out the new phase of relaxation.

2. Next, type the values of strength of voltage perturbation (-ve or +ve values), Vperturb, and the range of old phases for perturbation, Range_init and Range_final,
in the script 'PLAY.m'.
Description: Run PLAY.m will automatical set the remaining necessary variables and launch 'HHsimulate.m' to perform pertrubation analysis within the specified old phase
range, and it will return a phase response curve (PRC) for the specified strength of voltage pertrubation. Red-filled circular markers for the new phase in PRC plot
represents return of the system to the limit cycle and inform about the value of new phase. However, vertical black line in the PRC respresents collapse of the pertrubed
system to the steady-state.
